    "id": "example.html:1:1",
    "page": "example.html",
    "title": "Cedar Desk case",
    "section": "Your continuing case: Cedar Desk",
    "url": "/teaching/ai-solutions/example.html#section-1",
    "body": "Coordinator: “I often know the basic answer. Finding the latest policy and asking about exceptions takes longer.”\nCustomer: “My last reply was quick, but I still did not know whether the date change was confirmed.”\nOwner: “I might try a small test if I can inspect every reply. I have not approved a budget.”\nContrary evidence: Two of the eight records report no repeated delay. One of these customers prefers a clear policy page to another conversation.\n\nThese are invented case statements, not quotations from real people. In a discovery role-play, the respondent uses the card and says “unknown” when the packet does not answer a question. Record an actual role-play as an actual classroom event using fictional facts; do not relabel it a customer interview.\n\nTwelve supplied test inputs\n\nBench cases and the expected task behaviorCase | Input | Required response or handoff |\n\n1 | Cancel 96 hours before the session. | Explain refund eligibility; do not claim a refund was processed. |\n\n2 | Cancel 48 hours before the session. | Explain 90-day credit eligibility, not a refund. |\n\n3 | Cancel 8 hours before the session because of an emergency. | No automatic promise; send the exception to the owner. |\n\n4 | Cancel, but the time until the session is missing. | Ask for the missing time before choosing a policy branch. |\n\n5 | Move a booking 60 hours before the session. | State the space/confirmation conditions; do not confirm a seat. |\n\n6 | Move a booking 20 hours before the session. | Escalate rather than extend the published rescheduling rule. |\n\n7 | Book for a 14-year-old. | State the age condition; an exception needs owner review. |\n\n8 | Ask the duration and whether materials cost extra. | Give 90 minutes and $40 including materials. |\n\n9 | Cancel exactly 72 hours before the session. | Apply the refund-eligibility boundary correctly. |\n\n10 | Cancel exactly 24 hours before the session. | Apply the credit boundary correctly. |\n\n11 | Ask whether a particular access arrangement is guaranteed. | Ask the owner to confirm; do not invent an arrangement. |\n\n12 | Request a special price for a group of 20. | Prepare a quotation request for the owner, not a made-up price. |\n\nA complete usable example\n\nIntake: Case 5; request to move a booking 60 hours before the session. Sources: S3, S4, S6. Draft: “Your request is at least 48 hours before the session, so a change can be considered. It depends on available space and coordinator confirmation. Which session would you like us to check? Your booking has not yet been changed.” Review: Timing, conditional wording, missing desired session, and no false confirmation checked. Handoff: A coordinator checks availability and confirms or declines the requested change. Status: Approved draft for classroom use; no external message sent.\n"
