    "title": "Learning path",
    "section": "Your learning path",
    "url": "/teaching/ai-solutions/index.html#path-title",
    "body": "0 of 12 units complete\n\n01Find a worthwhile problem\nMilestone 1 · Problem and feasible scope\n\n01AI capabilities and useful tasks→\n02Discover the need→\n03Choose and scope a solution→\n02Make a usable solution\nMilestone 2 · First usable solution\n\n04Design the workflow→\n05Build the first usable version→\n03Test and improve\nMilestone 3 · Pilot and learning\n\n06Design a fair test→\n07Pilot, revise, and retest→\n04Prepare for real use\nMilestone 4 · Impact and adoption plan\n\n08Evaluate impact and resources→\n09Prepare the solution for use→\n10Bring the solution into use→\n05Deliver and defend\nMilestone 5 · Demonstration and handoff\n\n11Make delivery dependable→\n12Demonstrate and defend→\nWhat you need, what you will make, and how this works\nNo prerequisites. You do not need previous AI, programming, statistics, or business study. The lessons introduce the concepts and calculations as you use them.\nUse an ordinary document, presentation, or approved AI tool. No paid subscription or external business partner is required. The supplied Cedar Desk case provides a complete classroom starting point.\nEach unit follows Learn → Try → Build → Finish. Read the short assigned textbook scope inside Learn. Practice happens in the lesson. Build adds evidence to your project record. Marking a unit complete is a personal study marker, not a grade.\nYour final work includes a usable artifact or repeatable service, an observed user task, revision and retest, an impact and adoption plan, and operating instructions. A mock-up alone is not enough.\nAllow roughly 77–135 minutes per guided unit, including assigned textbook reading and its brief application, plus project work. The design estimate is 40–67 hours in total; actual timing and academic requirements depend on the offering. These estimates have not yet been validated with learners.\nUse your instructor’s dates and submission arrangements. Self-study does not include grading or a credential. See the five milestones."
